OTLP/HTTP test tools
=====================

Two Python scripts capture and replay the OTLP/HTTP traffic produced by the
test configurations.  Both scripts live in the test/ directory and need only
the Python standard library.


otlp_http-recorder.py
---------------------

A simple OTLP/HTTP receiver that records all incoming traffic to files.  It
listens for OTLP/HTTP POST requests on /v1/traces, /v1/metrics and /v1/logs
and appends each request (headers and body) to the output file of the matching
signal.  JSON bodies are written verbatim, or re-emitted with indentation when
--pretty is set; non-JSON bodies (e.g. protobuf) are written base64-encoded so
that the log file stays text-safe.  Bodies sent with Content-Encoding: gzip are
decompressed before recording.

When --upstream is given, every received request is also forwarded (teed) to
the configured OTLP/HTTP base URL in a background thread, so a real collector
keeps receiving the data while it is being recorded.

The server runs in the foreground or detaches as a daemon (--daemon), and it
reacts to signals: SIGUSR1 and SIGTERM shut it down gracefully, SIGHUP closes
and then reopens the output files (logrotate-friendly), and SIGUSR2 dumps a
per-endpoint statistics snapshot to stderr.  A statistics summary is written
to stderr on shutdown as well, and appended to the --stats-file when one is
given, which makes the numbers survive daemon mode.

Options:

  --daemon, -d            run in the background as a daemon
  --host, -H              IP address or hostname to bind to (default:
                          localhost)
  --logfile-logs, -l      output file for /v1/logs (default: <prefix>-logs)
  --logfile-metrics, -m   output file for /v1/metrics (default:
                          <prefix>-metrics)
  --logfile-traces, -r    output file for /v1/traces (default:
                          <prefix>-traces)
  --pidfile, -i           write the running PID to this file (removed on
                          shutdown)
  --port, -P              TCP port to listen on (default: 4318)
  --prefix, -x            prefix used to derive default log file names
                          (default: _otlp_http)
  --pretty, -p            pretty-print JSON request bodies in the log
  --quiet, -q             suppress the per-request access log on stderr
  --stats-file, -s        append SIGUSR2 and shutdown statistics to this file
  --timestamp, -t         include a UTC timestamp with each recorded request
  --upstream, -u          forward (tee) every received request to this
                          OTLP/HTTP base URL

The default port matches the OTLP/HTTP endpoint used by the test YAML files,
so the recorder can stand in for the collector while a test runs:

  % ./otlp_http-recorder.py --pretty --timestamp &
  % ./run-sa.sh


otlp_http-replay.py
-------------------

Replays OTLP/HTTP requests previously captured by otlp_http-recorder.py.  It
reads one or more recorded files and POSTs each captured request to the target
OTLP/HTTP base URL.  The headers that the HTTP client library manages (Host,
Content-Length, Connection, Accept-Encoding) are stripped before sending, and
Content-Encoding is stripped for records whose body was stored as plaintext
and preserved for records whose body was stored as base64.

Options:

  --delay, -d             seconds to sleep between requests (default: 0)
  --dry-run, -n           parse and report records without sending them
  --quiet, -q             suppress per-request status lines on stderr
  --target, -T            OTLP/HTTP base URL of the target collector
                          (default: http://localhost:4318)
  --timeout, -t           HTTP timeout in seconds for each request
                          (default: 10)

Example (replay a recorded trace file into a local collector):

  % ./otlp_http-replay.py --delay 0.1 _otlp_http-traces
